{
  "esm": "1.0.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "ReSEACT3DChem",
    "description": "ReSEACT 3-D transport on a NATIVE slice of a GEOS-FP grid, driven by real GEOS-FP meteorology. NO REGRIDDING: the local grid IS the native grid, so the whole conservative-overlap chain collapses to an index shift. The slice is (LON0, LAT0) + (NLON, NLAT, NLEV): local lon cell i is native LON0+i and local lat point j is native LAT0+j. The defaults 11/29 x 13x7x72 are lon centres -125..-65, lat points 26..50 -- the full CONUS span at 4x5 resolution, and the domain the runners simulate by default; LON0=14 with NLON=7 narrows it to -110..-80, the smaller central-US box the model was first developed on.\\n\\nRESOLUTION. Every parameter default in this file is 4x5, but nothing in the model is welded to it: the forcing arrays are declared over the SYMBOLIC dims gf_lon/gf_lat/gf_lev/gf_t and sized from the files at bind time, and the four numbers that do carry a resolution -- lon0_deg, lat0_deg, dlon_deg, dlat_deg -- are parameters (dlon_deg/dlat_deg were literal equations until 2026-09-05, which is what made 4x5 structural rather than merely default). `native_slice(res = \\\"2x2.5\\\")` in prototypes/reseact_3d_chem/split_common.jl overrides all four, the LON0/LAT0/NLON/NLAT metaparameters and the GEOS-FP URL together from one table row; the rows are 4x5, 2x2.5, 0.25x0.3125 and 0.25x0.3125_CH, and the same CONUS footprint is 13x7 columns at 4x5, 25x13 at 2x2.5 and 193x97 at 0.25x0.3125. The vertical is 72 hybrid levels in ALL of them, so dA/dB/Ap/Bp and NLEV never change with the horizontal.\n\nTransport is the monotone flux-form PPM stack imported BY REFERENCE from EarthSciDiscretizations: regional open-wall inflow BCs on lon/lat, and the HYBRID sigma-pressure massform vertical (dp = dA[k] + dB[k]*PS(i,j,t)) on lev. The horizontal and vertical rule stack is byte-identical to the verified prototypes/transport_3d/transport_3d.esm; only the FORCING differs — the analytic test winds and constant PS are replaced by GEOS-FP U/V/OMEGA/PS, each supplied by an EarthSciIO discrete_provider returning the TWO records bracketing the solver time and blended continuously in time (records_per_sample=2 + w_time, the era5 pattern).\n\nGEOS-FP's collections are not co-phased, so three independent weights are used: w_I3 (PS, 3-hourly instantaneous at 00:00Z), w_A3 (U/V/OMEGA, 3-hourly means at 01:30Z) and w_A1 (PBLH, hourly means at 00:30Z).\n\nFace fluxes follow the metric the ESD divergence stencils imply on the unit sphere: Mx = dp_face*U_face/a and My = dp_face*V_face/a (Pa/s, matching m in Pa), with the A-grid GEOS-FP winds averaged to faces. Mz is NOT taken from GEOS-FP's OMEGA. It is DIAGNOSED from continuity, the standard offline-CTM construction: integrating dm_k/dt = -(divh_fix_k + Mz[k+1] - Mz[k]) upward from a rigid ground with the requirement that m reproduce the hydrostatic thickness gives Mz[ke] = -sum_{k<ke}(dB[k]*dPSdt + divh_fix[k]), +ve UPWARD at each cell's LOWER edge. Mz[1] = 0 falls out as the empty sum and Mz[NLEV+1] = 0 because sum_k dB[k] = 1 and the pressure fixer makes sum_k divh_fix[k] = -dPSdt exactly -- so the diagnosed field satisfies the no-flux walls the massform stencils already assume, rather than merely being clipped by them. Using OMEGA directly instead is what made m drift from dp; see caveat (4).\n\nCHEMISTRY + PHOTOLYSIS. The SuperFast gas-phase mechanism (EarthSciModels components/gaschem/superfast.esm) is lifted onto the slice by operator_compose with pointwise lifting, and its six photolysis rates come from the Fast-JX component (components/gaschem/fastjx/fastjx_interp_troposphere.esm) rather than being fixed numbers.\n\nWHICH Fast-JX, and why. The fastjx/ directory holds two variants. fastjx.esm scales each of its 18 actinic-flux bands as max(0, cos_sza * const_i), with no pressure dependence at all; fastjx_interp_troposphere.esm interpolates the flux bilinearly on a real (cos_sza, P) table, which gives it genuine vertical structure, and that is the one mounted here. Since stage 1 of the 2026-10-28 stack sync it matches jialinl6/GasChemMTK.jl@37792b29 (branch superfast-gas-only): it computes the solar position itself from lat, long and the UTC clock t + t_ref (NOAA Spencer equation of time), rather than taking cos_sza as an input. Its calendar runs datetime.* on t + t_ref, which EarthSciAST evaluates as branch-free arithmetic (ess-traced-datetime, 2026-08-06), so it takes the solver time and traces under Reactant -- the same calendar NEIRegrid's local-hour clock uses.\n\nSo Transport3D no longer carries a solar chain. It hands FastJX the per-cell latitude and longitude (lat_c, lon_c, from the grid's own lat_coord / lon_coord templates) and the model epoch t_ref = 2016-01-01T00:00:00Z, which makes t + t_ref the UTC clock; the sun moves with the solve.\n\nFast-JX has no ODEs, so it is NOT operator_composed; it is lifted purely by SHAPE. Six param_to_var edges hand it its inputs — lat_c, lon_c, Tc and Pc (the same GEOS-FP temperature and hybrid mid-layer pressure SuperFast reads) and H2Oc, all grid-shaped, plus the scalar epoch t_ref — and promote_downstream_shapes then carries [lon,lat,lev] through the whole flux -> cross-section -> j-rate chain. Six more edges map j_H2O2 / j_H2COa / j_H2COb / j_CH3OOH / j_NO2 / j_o32OH onto SuperFast's jH2O2 / jH2COa / jH2COb / jCH3OOH / jNO2 / jO32OH (the wiring of the EarthSciModels coupling library couplings/fastjx_superfast.esm, spelled out inline — see the first of those edges for why). The splitter's stencil_vs_pointwise rule puts the entire photolysis chain in the POINTWISE half, alongside the chemistry it drives.\n\nDEPOSITION AND EMISSIONS, as of the 2026-10-28 stack sync. Dry deposition is EarthSciModels' wesley_dry_gas.esm = jialinl6/DepositionMTK.jl@cbe7d7db (branch superfast-gas-only) DryDepositionGasFractional: the Wesely mosaic over 11 land-use classes, whose fractions f_<class> come from the 0.05 deg MODIS land use (EarthSciModels wesely_landuse.esm) area-averaged onto each cell by LandUseRemap, as upstream's _remap_landuse does; the season comes from the UTC month (season_c), and z / del_P are upstream's corrected first-level midpoint height and thickness. CH2O deposits with k_CH2O, and NO is not dry-deposited. NEI2016 is EarthSciML/EarthSciData.jl PR #226 (head jialinl6:Oct_merge @87f8da1): the merged surface file plus the six inline point sectors (mounted as NEI2016Elevated), all regridded by NEIRegrid; the point sectors are spread over layers 1-11 by GEOS-Chem's per-sector profiles, NOx and isoprene follow the round(lon/15) clock, and NO converts with MW_NO2. Each layer's emission is divided by its instantaneous thickness dp (the air the transport carries), where upstream uses a time-mean dry thickness table.\n\nCAVEATS. (1) PS is read in hPa and scaled by 100 — the loader declares Pa but EarthSciIO returns raw file values. (2) The lev no-flux BC is baked into the stencils (they never read Mz[1] or Mz[NLEV+1]), so the 7-level slab is CLOSED at its ~906 hPa top even though real air flows through it — a truncated-column artifact, negligible over the 1 s first-light window but not for long runs. (3) The two end lat rows are half-width (lat_edge clamps them) while native lat cells are a full 4 deg. (4) PRESSURE FIXER, and the mass source it implies. GEOS-FP's A3 winds are 3-hourly MEANS while its I3 surface pressure is INSTANTANEOUS, so the two disagree: the column-integrated horizontal mass divergence divh_col does not equal -dPSdt, and the discrete identity d(dp)/dt + div(M) = 0 fails (Jockel et al. 2001). Left alone, m wandered away from dp = dA[k] + dB[k]*PS at ~3e-6 1/s and the long runs died of it. The fixer is COLUMN-LOCAL: divh_fix = divh + (-dPSdt - divh_col)*dp/dp_col spreads the column imbalance over the levels by dp weight, Mz is diagnosed from divh_fix, and -- this is the part that has to match -- the CONTINUITY equation reads the same divh_fix. Pairing a divh_fix-derived Mz with the raw divergence leaves the correction uncancelled and reproduces the drift almost exactly; that was a real bug, caught by tools/continuity_residual.jl, whose signature is a residual that is CONSTANT down each column in dp-relative terms. With both sides consistent, dm_k/dt = dB[k]*dPSdt to roundoff -- measured 1.3e-17 1/s rms (max 3.5e-16) on the RHS itself over a 7x7x72 slice, and integrating for 30 simulated minutes leaves max|m - dp|/dp at 2.6e-13, growing SUBLINEARLY across the six macro steps (1.05, 1.77, 1.98, 2.21, 2.54, 2.61 e-13), which is bounded accumulation roundoff rather than a residual bias. Note what this does and does not buy. The column budget is OVER-DETERMINED -- sum_k dm_k/dt is pinned to -divh_col by the no-flux top and bottom walls whatever Mz does, while the target sums to dPSdt -- so making m track dp REQUIRES a mass source equal to that imbalance (~4e-6 1/s here, ~1.3% of column mass per hour). This form ABSORBS it rather than eliminating it. Eliminating it means adjusting Mx and My so divh_col == -dPSdt, a 2-D Poisson solve for a velocity potential, which is not a column-local operation and is not what this is. Composition is untouched either way: the species stay in CWC mixing-ratio form with the RAW div(M), which is exactly the mixing-ratio equation implied by carrying the source at the local mixing ratio, so the correction cancels out of dq/dt identically. CWC is verified, not asserted: tools/cwc_gate.jl synthesises a gate copy of this model and finds max|q-1| == 0 EXACTLY in both the mixing-ratio form the species use and the mass form mqg/mg, with tracer mass bitwise equal to air mass throughout, and each of the three axes' tracer-vs-continuity divergence difference exactly 0.0 at every cell -- including through the diagnosed Mz. (The composite three-axis sum shows ~1e-17, which the per-axis states prove is the association order of two sums of bitwise-identical addends in the emitted code, not the operator.) (5) FastJX.H2O is the REAL 3-D field (Transport3D.Qc, from GEOS-FP I3 specific humidity QV). It started life as a single tropospheric scalar and that turned out to be fatal, not cosmetic: the O(1D)+H2O -> 2OH branching depends on the H2O MIXING RATIO alone, so a constant 1.84% overstates the OH yield ~3,320x above 12 km and drove a HOx runaway that killed the solver 20 simulated minutes into a dawn run at NLEV=72. NOTE the remaining inconsistency: SuperFast's own H2O is still a constant 1.839e7 ppb reservoir, so the mechanism and the photolysis now disagree about humidity aloft. Coupling Transport3D.Qc -> SuperFast.H2O would close that, but it changes the MECHANISM's water, not just a photolysis input. (6) Fast-JX here is the TROPOSPHERIC table. Its (cos_sza, P) flux lookup already has a CLIMATOLOGICAL clear-sky overhead-ozone column baked in — that is exactly what makes it usable at the surface, and why its j_O31D at overhead sun (3.4e-5 1/s, the textbook value) is ~206x smaller than the sibling fastjx.esm's unattenuated max(0, cos_sza*const_i) form (6.9e-3 1/s, a top-of-atmosphere number). What is NOT modelled is any VARIATION about that climatology: no prognostic O3 column, no cloud, no aerosol, no surface albedo. The P axis spans 1000..100000 Pa, so the surface layer at ~101325 Pa clamps to the last column (a 1.3% extrapolation declined); every level above is interpolated. (7) The upstream fastjx_interp_troposphere.esm carried a trivial `NO2` state with D(NO2) = -j_NO2*NO2 purely as an \"ODE skeleton\" for its example plots. That state is incompatible with mounting the component on a grid — once cos_sza/T/P arrive per cell every observed is promoted to [lon,lat,lev], leaving a SCALAR state with an array-valued RHS — so it was removed upstream, making the component stateless like its sibling fastjx.esm. Its three inline tests assert only J-rates and F_i at t=0 and are unaffected. (8) The tables' ABSOLUTE calibration is inherited, not audited here: at overhead sun they give j_NO2 = 2.1e-3 1/s, where the commonly cited clear-sky noon value is nearer 8e-3 1/s (the sibling fastjx.esm gives 3.9e-3). j_O31D, by contrast, lands on the textbook value. Both components come from the same GasChem.jl migration, so a low j_NO2 normalisation is an UPSTREAM question, not an artifact of this model's wiring — flagged because photolysis of NO2 sets the NOx-O3 photostationary state and therefore most of the O3 response seen here.",
    "license": "MIT",
    "tags": [
      "esd:problem",
      "grid:latlon3d",
      "family:finite_volume",
      "op:D",
      "axes:lon,lat,lev",
      "scheme:flux_form",
      "property:free_stream",
      "bc:inflow"
    ]
  },
  "metaparameters": {
    "NLON": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 13,
      "description": "Zonal cell count of the native GEOS-FP slice (east halo native LON0+NLON+1 <= NLON_native: 1..57 at 4x5, 1..121 at 2x2.5). Bound at load; folds the transport template dims. The default 13, with LON0=11 and the 4x5 defaults, spans lon -125..-65: CONUS, which is the domain the runners simulate by default. The SAME footprint is (LON0,NLON) = (22,25) at 2x2.5 and (176,193) at 0.25x0.3125; `native_slice` carries that per-resolution CONUS box so a resolution switch does not move the domain."
    },
    "NLAT": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 7,
      "description": "Meridional cell count of the slice (top lat point native LAT0+NLAT <= NLAT_native: 1..17 at 4x5 with LAT0=29, and the same rule at every other resolution). Drives the lat-edge clamp min(gje,NLAT)."
    },
    "NLEV": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 72,
      "description": "Vertical level count (1..72: hybrid-coef table length). Drives the lev-edge clamp min(gke,NLEV); a value <72 is a truncated column (dA/dB sliced by the host)."
    },
    "LON0": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 11,
      "description": "Zonal origin of the native GEOS-FP slice: local lon cell i is native cell LON0+i, so at 4x5 LON0=11 puts i=1 at native 12 (centre -125, the west edge of CONUS) and LON0=14 puts it at native 15 (centre -110, the old central-US box). Bound at load. Must satisfy LON0 >= 1 (the west halo reads native LON0) and LON0+NLON+1 <= NLON_native (the east halo) -- 72 at 4x5, 144 at 2x2.5, 1152 at 0.25x0.3125. The `Transport3D.lon0_deg` PARAMETER carries the same origin in degrees and must be overridden to match: lon0_deg = lon_first + dlon_deg*LON0 - dlon_deg/2 (-182.5 + 5*LON0 at 4x5). `native_slice(res=...)` in prototypes/reseact_3d_chem/split_common.jl derives every one of these from a single resolution row and is the only supported way to leave 4x5."
    },
    "LAT0": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 29,
      "description": "Meridional origin of the slice: local lat point j is native point LAT0+j, so at 4x5 LAT0=29 puts j=1 at native 30 (26 degN). Bound at load. Must satisfy LAT0 >= 1 (the south flank reads native LAT0) and LAT0+NLAT <= NLAT_native -- 46 at 4x5, 91 at 2x2.5, 721 at 0.25x0.3125. The `Transport3D.lat0_deg` PARAMETER carries the same origin in degrees and must be overridden to match: lat0_deg = lat_first + dlat_deg*LAT0 (-90 + 4*LAT0 at 4x5)."
    },
    "GF_NLON": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 72,
      "description": "Zonal extent of the NATIVE GEOS-FP arrays the forcing parameters are delivered at: 72 at 4x5, 144 at 2x2.5, 1152 at 0.25x0.3125, 225 at 0.25x0.3125_CH. Sizes the gf_lon index set, which appears ONLY as a shape dim of the F_* forcing parameters -- never as an aggregate range -- so it is a DECLARATION of what the loader hands over, not something the emitted indexing depends on (verified: masking every native cell outside the model's halo window leaves the RHS bit-identical at both 4x5 and 2x2.5, tools/diag/native_window_probe.jl). It was the literal 72 until 2026-09-05, which was simply false at any other resolution. `native_slice(res=...)` sets it from the same row that picks the URL."
    },
    "GF_NLAT": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 46,
      "description": "Meridional extent of the native GEOS-FP arrays: 46 at 4x5, 91 at 2x2.5, 721 at 0.25x0.3125, 161 at 0.25x0.3125_CH. Sizes gf_lat; see GF_NLON."
    },
    "NEI_NX": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 459,
      "description": "Column count of the NEI2016 12US1 source grid (IOAPI NCOLS). Fixed by the data product, not by the run. Ties the source ring builder's GX, the NSRC index-set size, and the (row, col) flattening of the native array to one number."
    },
    "NEI_NY": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 299,
      "description": "Row count of the NEI2016 12US1 source grid (IOAPI NROWS)."
    },
    "LU_NX": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 1400,
      "description": "Land-use file columns (0.05 deg, -130..-60)."
    },
    "LU_NY": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 700,
      "description": "Land-use file rows (0.05 deg, 20..55)."
    },
    "LU_WX": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 101,
      "description": "Land-use columns a target cell can touch: ceil(dlon_deg/0.05) + 1 (101 at 4x5). native_slice sets it per resolution."
    },
    "LU_WY": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 81,
      "description": "Land-use rows a target cell can touch: ceil(dlat_deg/0.05) + 1 (81 at 4x5). native_slice sets it per resolution."
    }
  },
  "models": {
    "GEOSFP": {
      "ref": "../EarthSciModels/components/earthsci_data/geosfp.esm"
    },
    "FastJX": {
      "ref": "../EarthSciModels/components/gaschem/fastjx/fastjx_interp_troposphere.esm"
    },
    "Transport3D": {
      "expression_template_imports": [
        {
          "ref": "../EarthSciDiscretizations/grids/latlon3d/rules/face_flux_divergence_lon_open_bc.esm",
          "bindings": {
            "NLON": "NLON",
            "NLAT": "NLAT",
            "NLEV": "NLEV"
          }
        },
        {
          "ref": "../EarthSciDiscretizations/grids/latlon3d/rules/face_flux_divergence_lat_open_bc.esm",
          "bindings": {
            "NLON": "NLON",
            "NLAT": "NLAT",
            "NLEV": "NLEV"
          }
        },
        {
          "ref": "../EarthSciDiscretizations/grids/latlon3d/rules/ppm_flux_D_lon_mono_inflow_bc.esm",
          "bindings": {
            "NLON": "NLON",
            "NLAT": "NLAT",
            "NLEV": "NLEV"
          }
        },
        {
          "ref": "../EarthSciDiscretizations/grids/latlon3d/rules/ppm_flux_D_lat_mono_inflow_bc.esm",
          "bindings": {
            "NLON": "NLON",
            "NLAT": "NLAT",
            "NLEV": "NLEV"
          }
        },
        {
          "ref": "../EarthSciDiscretizations/grids/latlon3d/rules/ppm_flux_D_lev_mono_hybrid_noflux_bc.esm",
          "bindings": {
            "NLON": "NLON",
            "NLAT": "NLAT",
            "NLEV": "NLEV"
          }
        },
        {
          "ref": "../EarthSciDiscretizations/grids/latlon3d/rules/face_flux_divergence_lev_massform_noflux_bc.esm",
          "bindings": {
            "NLON": "NLON",
            "NLAT": "NLAT",
            "NLEV": "NLEV"
          }
        }
      ],
      "variables": {
        "m": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "Pa",
          "shape": [
            "lon",
            "lat",
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Air mass per cell (a pressure thickness). Strictly positive."
        },
        "lon0_deg": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "deg",
          "default": -127.5,
          "description": "West EDGE of the slice, in degrees. GEOS-FP lon cell edges are lon_first + dlon_deg*(i-1) - dlon_deg/2, so this MUST be kept equal to lon_first + dlon_deg*LON0 - dlon_deg/2 (-182.5 + 5*LON0 on the 4x5 grid) -- it is the degree-space twin of the LON0 metaparameter and nothing in the model derives one from the other (a parameter default cannot be an expression over a metaparameter, and dlon_deg is itself only a parameter). The default -127.5 is the edge of native 4x5 cell i=12, matching LON0=11 (CONUS). `native_slice(res=...)` in split_common.jl derives all four -- lon0_deg, lat0_deg, dlon_deg, dlat_deg -- from one origin and one resolution row; set them by hand and they can disagree silently, which shifts the SOLAR ZENITH ANGLE off the transported air without shifting the meteorology."
        },
        "lat0_deg": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "deg",
          "default": 26.0,
          "description": "Southern-most lat POINT of the slice, in degrees. GEOS-FP lat points are lat_first + dlat_deg*(j-1), so this MUST be kept equal to lat_first + dlat_deg*LAT0 (-90 + 4*LAT0 on the 4x5 grid) -- the degree-space twin of the LAT0 metaparameter (see lon0_deg for why it is not derived). The default 26.0 is native 4x5 point j=30, matching LAT0=29."
        },
        "dlon_deg": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "deg",
          "default": 5.0,
          "description": "Native zonal cell width of the GEOS-FP grid this run is driven by. A PARAMETER, not a literal: it was `dlon_deg = 5.0` as an equation until resolution became a first-class argument, and an equation cannot be overridden from a runner, so the whole model was welded to 4x5. The default is still 4x5 so a bare `EA.load_path(model)` is unchanged; `native_slice(res=...)` in prototypes/reseact_3d_chem/split_common.jl sets it -- together with lon0_deg, dlat_deg and NEIRegrid's copies of all four -- from the ONE resolution row that also picks the GEOS-FP URL. Consumed by EarthSciDiscretizations' latlon geometry templates (cell edges, areas, the metric in Mx = dp_face*U_face/a) in ordinary arithmetic, exactly as lon0_deg already was. Open west/east walls (regional, not periodic)."
        },
        "dlat_deg": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "deg",
          "default": 4.0,
          "description": "Native meridional point spacing of the GEOS-FP grid this run is driven by; the twin of dlon_deg, see there for why it is a parameter rather than the literal 4.0 it used to be. Open south/north walls (regional, not polar)."
        },
        "phie": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "deg",
          "shape": [
            "lat_nodes"
          ],
          "description": "Latitude cell EDGES from the grid's lat_edge template; the half-polar caps fall out of the clamp at +/-90."
        },
        "coslat_e": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lat_nodes"
          ],
          "description": "cos of the latitude edges. Zero-length face at each pole. Never read at faces 1 or NLAT+1 -- the pole terms are structurally omitted."
        },
        "dS_lat": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lat"
          ],
          "description": "Exact spherical cell-area weight sin(phie[j+1]) - sin(phie[j])."
        },
        "dphi_lat": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "rad",
          "shape": [
            "lat"
          ],
          "description": "Meridional cell extent; HALF-width at the two polar caps."
        },
        "Mx": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "Pa/s",
          "shape": [
            "lon_nodes",
            "lat",
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Zonal face-normal AIR-MASS flux (Pa/s) at each lon face: Mx = dp_face * U_face / a, the metric the ESD divergence stencil implies on the unit sphere (cell area dlon_rad*dS_lat, zonal face length dphi_lat). dp_face and U_face are 2-point averages of the two native cells flanking the face (LON0-1+ie, LON0+ie) — exact, because each local lon cell has the same footprint as native cell LON0+i and the local edges sit on native cell midpoints. Replaces the Stage-B analytic wind. The west/east wall faces (ie=1, ie=NLON+1) are GENUINE faces on an open domain, and their halo cells (native LON0 and LON0+NLON+1) exist in the native array — no extrapolation needed."
        },
        "My": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "Pa/s",
          "shape": [
            "lon",
            "lat_nodes",
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Meridional face-normal AIR-MASS flux (Pa/s) at each lat face: My = dp_face * V_face / a, the metric the ESD divergence stencil implies on the unit sphere (meridional face length dlon_rad*cos(phi), supplied by the stencil's coslat_e factor). Because lat is POINT-based with lat_edge CLAMPING the two end edges onto the end points, the flanking native points are jL = (LAT0-1)+max(je,2) and jR = LAT0+min(je,NLAT): the walls (je=1, je=NLAT+1) collapse to the single native point that the face sits on, and interior faces bisect their two neighbours. Replaces the Stage-B analytic wind."
        },
        "dA": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "Pa",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "default": 0.0,
          "description": "dA[k] = Ap[k]-Ap[k+1] from the native GEOS-FP hybrid table at integer edges k=1..8. Supplied via const_arrays."
        },
        "dB": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "default": 0.0,
          "description": "dB[k] = Bp[k]-Bp[k+1] from the native GEOS-FP hybrid table at integer edges k=1..8. Supplied via const_arrays."
        },
        "PS": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "Pa",
          "shape": [
            "lon",
            "lat"
          ],
          "description": "Surface pressure (Pa) on the local grid: the GEOS-FP I3 field at NATIVE (lat=LAT0+j, lon=LON0+i), blended across its two bracketing records with w_I3 and scaled hPa->Pa. Replaces the Stage-B constant 101325. Drives dp = dA[k] + dB[k]*PS, so the hybrid vertical breathes with the real surface pressure."
        },
        "dp": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "Pa",
          "shape": [
            "lon",
            "lat",
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Physical pressure thickness dp = dA[k] + dB[k]*Ps(i,j,t) on the GEOS-FP hybrid sigma-pressure grid; the CW84 parabola is built on these (ppmflux_D_lev_hyb_*). dA/dB are differences of the native GEOS-FP Ap(Pa)/Bp tables at integer edges k=1..8 -- read directly, NOT via interp.linear, which keeps the transport RHS pure arithmetic and therefore autodiff-capable."
        },
        "dPSdt": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "Pa/s",
          "shape": [
            "lon",
            "lat"
          ],
          "description": "Surface-pressure tendency, (PS_2 - PS_1)/dt_I3, from the TWO bracketing I3 records already in the forcing buffer (records_per_sample=2) and scaled hPa->Pa like PS itself. This is the tendency the column-integrated mass divergence MUST reproduce; the whole pressure fixer exists because it does not."
        },
        "divh": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "Pa/s",
          "shape": [
            "lon",
            "lat",
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Horizontal air-mass flux divergence, D(Mx,lon) + D(My,lat) -- the same two unlowered rewrite targets the D(m) equation carries, so the imported ESD open-BC rules lower them identically here."
        },
        "divh_col": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "Pa/s",
          "shape": [
            "lon",
            "lat"
          ],
          "description": "Column-integrated horizontal divergence, sum_k divh(k). Contracting `gk` (present in expr, absent from output_idx) is the ordinary aggregate reduction."
        },
        "dp_col": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "Pa",
          "shape": [
            "lon",
            "lat"
          ],
          "description": "Column mass sum_k dp(k) = PS - P_top. The weight the pressure-fixer correction is distributed by, so the correction is MASS-weighted rather than uniform in k."
        },
        "divh_fix": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "Pa/s",
          "shape": [
            "lon",
            "lat",
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Pressure-FIXED horizontal divergence:\n    divh_fix(k) = divh(k) + (-dPSdt - divh_col) * dp(k)/dp_col\nGEOS-FP archives U/V/OMEGA as 3-hourly MEANS but PS as an INSTANTANEOUS field, so the discrete identity d(dp)/dt + div(M) = 0 does not hold and the column budget misses by (dPSdt + divh_col). Measured on this model: the air mass drifts from its own hydrostatic thickness at a CONSTANT rate -- 4.6e-3 rms per 300 s step at 21x13x72, dead linear, worst cells at k=1 -- until a cell reaches zero and the solve dies (3.6-5.2 h).\n\nThe residual is spread over the column in proportion to dp, so the correction is mass-weighted and vanishes where there is no mass. This is the COLUMN-LOCAL fixer: it enforces sum_k divh_fix = -dPSdt exactly, per column, with no global coupling. The variational (Poisson) fixer of Jockel et al. (2001) would distribute the same total correction more smoothly in the horizontal, but it is a global elliptic solve -- neither pointwise nor a stencil, so the splitter could not separate it and a Reactant trace would have to carry an iterative solver. The column-local form removes the drain without any of that."
        },
        "Mz": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "Pa/s",
          "shape": [
            "lon",
            "lat",
            "lev_nodes"
          ],
          "description": "Vertical face-normal AIR-MASS flux (Pa/s) at each cell's LOWER edge, +ve UPWARD, DIAGNOSED FROM CONTINUITY rather than read from GEOS-FP OMEGA:\n    Mz[ke] = - sum_{k < ke} ( dB[k]*dPSdt + divh_fix(k) )\nan exclusive forward scan over lev (esm-spec 4.3.1 cumulative reductions; filter j < i). Mz[1] = 0 falls out as the empty-set additive identity -- no flux through the ground -- and Mz[NLEV+1] = 0 falls out BECAUSE divh_fix makes the column budget close. So both no-flux walls the facediv_lev_mass_nf_k1/_kN stencils assume are now SATISFIED rather than merely asserted.\n\nThis replaces Mz = -OMEGA. Taking OMEGA directly is what made m drift from dp (Jockel et al. 2001): OMEGA is a 3-hourly MEAN while PS is INSTANTANEOUS, so the two never closed the same budget. Diagnosing Mz instead means the vertical flux CANNOT be inconsistent -- it is defined as whatever closes continuity. Note F_OM is consequently no longer read by the dynamics."
        },
        "F_PS": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "hPa",
          "shape": [
            "gf_t",
            "gf_lat",
            "gf_lon"
          ],
          "default": 0.0,
          "description": "GEOS-FP I3 surface pressure on the NATIVE 4x5 grid, the two records bracketing the current time: [time,lat,lon] = [gf_t,gf_lat,gf_lon]. Coupled from GEOSFP.GEOSFP_I3.PS. UNITS ARE hPa, not the Pa the loader declares — the file says hPa and EarthSciIO returns raw values, so every consumer here scales by 100."
        },
        "F_U": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "m/s",
          "shape": [
            "gf_t",
            "gf_lev",
            "gf_lat",
            "gf_lon"
          ],
          "default": 0.0,
          "description": "GEOS-FP A3dyn eastward wind, two bracketing records, NATIVE [time,lev,lat,lon]. Coupled from GEOSFP.GEOSFP_A3dyn.U. CELL-CENTRED (A-grid) despite the loader's 'x-edge-staggered' note — the archived GEOS-Chem files are A-grid — so it is averaged to lon faces here."
        },
        "F_V": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "m/s",
          "shape": [
            "gf_t",
            "gf_lev",
            "gf_lat",
            "gf_lon"
          ],
          "default": 0.0,
          "description": "GEOS-FP A3dyn northward wind, two bracketing records, NATIVE [time,lev,lat,lon]. Coupled from GEOSFP.GEOSFP_A3dyn.V. Cell-centred; averaged to lat faces here."
        },
        "F_OM": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "Pa/s",
          "shape": [
            "gf_t",
            "gf_lev",
            "gf_lat",
            "gf_lon"
          ],
          "default": 0.0,
          "description": "GEOS-FP A3dyn vertical pressure velocity OMEGA = dP/dt, +ve DOWNWARD, two bracketing records, NATIVE [time,lev,lat,lon]. Coupled from GEOSFP.GEOSFP_A3dyn.OMEGA. Cell-centred; averaged to lev edges and NEGATED to give Mz (+ve upward)."
        },
        "F_PBLH": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "m",
          "shape": [
            "gf_t",
            "gf_lat",
            "gf_lon"
          ],
          "default": 0.0,
          "description": "GEOS-FP A1 planetary boundary layer height, two bracketing records, NATIVE [time,lat,lon]. Coupled from GEOSFP.GEOSFP_A1.PBLH. Drives the full PBL mixing operator."
        },
        "w_I3": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "default": 0.0,
          "description": "Time-interpolation weight for the I3 cadence (PS): 3-hourly INSTANTANEOUS records at 00:00, 03:00, ... UTC. Coupled from GEOSFP.w_time_I3."
        },
        "w_A3": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "default": 0.0,
          "description": "Time-interpolation weight for the A3 cadence (U/V/OMEGA): 3-hourly time-AVERAGED records centred 01:30, 04:30, ... UTC. Coupled from GEOSFP.w_time_A3. Distinct from w_I3 because GEOS-FP's collections are not co-phased."
        },
        "w_A1": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "default": 0.0,
          "description": "Time-interpolation weight for the A1 cadence (PBLH): hourly time-AVERAGED records centred 00:30, 01:30, ... UTC. Coupled from GEOSFP.w_time_A1."
        },
        "PBLH": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m",
          "shape": [
            "lon",
            "lat"
          ],
          "description": "Planetary boundary layer height (m) on the local grid: the GEOS-FP A1 field at NATIVE (lat=LAT0+j, lon=LON0+i), blended across its two bracketing records with w_A1. Drives the GEOS-Chem-style full PBL mixing via p_pbl_top -> pbl_f."
        },
        "F_T": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "K",
          "shape": [
            "gf_t",
            "gf_lev",
            "gf_lat",
            "gf_lon"
          ],
          "default": 0.0,
          "description": "GEOS-FP I3 temperature, two bracketing records, NATIVE [time,lev,lat,lon]. Coupled from GEOSFP.GEOSFP_I3.T. Declared K and delivered K — unlike PS, no unit gap here (verified against the file)."
        },
        "Tc": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "K",
          "shape": [
            "lon",
            "lat",
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Air temperature (K) on the local grid: the GEOS-FP I3 field at NATIVE (lev=k, lat=LAT0+j, lon=LON0+i), blended across its two bracketing records with w_I3. Coupled into SuperFast.T; param_to_var carries this grid shape onto the scalar param so the pointwise lift indexes it per cell."
        },
        "Ap": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "Pa",
          "shape": [
            "lev_nodes"
          ],
          "default": 0.0,
          "description": "GEOS-FP hybrid sigma-pressure Ap coefficients at the NLEV+1 layer edges (Pa). k=1 is the surface (Ap[1]=0, Bp[1]=1 => P_edge(1)=PS). Supplied via const_arrays."
        },
        "Bp": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lev_nodes"
          ],
          "default": 0.0,
          "description": "GEOS-FP hybrid sigma-pressure Bp coefficients at the NLEV+1 layer edges (dimensionless). Supplied via const_arrays."
        },
        "Pc": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "Pa",
          "shape": [
            "lon",
            "lat",
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Air pressure (Pa) at each layer's mid-point: the mean of its two hybrid sigma-pressure edges, P_edge(k) = Ap[k] + Bp[k]*PS(i,j,t). Breathes with the real surface pressure. Coupled into SuperFast.P."
        },
        "latp": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "deg",
          "shape": [
            "lat"
          ],
          "description": "Latitude of each lat POINT, lat_j = lat0_deg + (j-1)*dlat_deg, from the latlon grid's own lat_coord template (the same producer lat_edge folds from). Pure geometry, no forcing. Feeds lat_c (FastJX.lat)."
        },
        "lonp": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "deg",
          "shape": [
            "lon"
          ],
          "description": "Longitude of each lon cell CENTRE, lon_i = lon0_deg + (i-1/2)*dlon_deg, from the latlon grid's own lon_coord template (lon0_deg is the west EDGE, hence the half-cell shift). Feeds lon_c (FastJX.long)."
        },
        "t_ref": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "s",
          "default": 1451606400.0,
          "description": "Reference epoch (Unix s) that model time t is measured from: 2016-01-01T00:00:00Z, the EPOCH_DATE of split_common.jl. MUST equal NEIRegrid.t_ref. Coupled into FastJX.t_ref, so the photolysis component reads the UTC clock t + t_ref, and used for the deposition season."
        },
        "lat_c": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "deg",
          "shape": [
            "lon",
            "lat",
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Latitude (deg) of each cell, latp[j] broadcast over lon and lev. Coupled into FastJX.lat, as jialinl6/GasChemMTK.jl@37792b29 (branch superfast-gas-only) couple2(FastJXCoupler, GEOSFPCoupler) maps f.lat ~ rad2deg(g.lat). Shaped [lon,lat,lev] so every FastJX input (lat, long, T, P, H2O) carries the same axes and the shape promotion that lifts the 0-D photolysis chain never aligns differently-shaped operands by name."
        },
        "lon_c": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "deg",
          "shape": [
            "lon",
            "lat",
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Longitude (deg) of each cell centre, lonp[i] broadcast over lat and lev. Coupled into FastJX.long (f.long ~ rad2deg(g.lon) upstream)."
        },
        "season_c": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "description": "Wesely (1989) season index from the UTC month of t + t_ref, as DepositionMTK's season_at does for the Northern-Hemisphere mid-latitudes: Jun-Aug 1 (midsummer), Sep-Oct 2 (autumn), Nov 3 (late autumn), Dec-Feb 4 (winter), Mar-May 5 (transitional). Coupled into DryDepositionGas.season."
        },
        "F_RH": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "gf_t",
            "gf_lev",
            "gf_lat",
            "gf_lon"
          ],
          "default": 0.0,
          "description": "GEOS-FP A3dyn relative humidity w.r.t. liquid water (dimensionless 0-1), two bracketing records, NATIVE [time,lev,lat,lon]. Coupled from GEOSFP.GEOSFP_A3dyn.RH. A3dyn cadence, so it blends with w_A3 (the 3-hourly MEANS at 01:30Z), NOT w_I3."
        },
        "H2Oc": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "ppb",
          "shape": [
            "lon",
            "lat",
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Water vapour volume mixing ratio (ppb), computed EXACTLY as the reference implementation does. GasChem.jl's `couple2(SuperFastCoupler, GEOSFPCoupler)` and `couple2(FastJXCoupler, GEOSFPCoupler)` (ext/EarthSciDataExt.jl) both set H2O via\n    water_concentration_ppb(RH, p, T):\n      Tc   = T - 273.15\n      es   = 6.112 * exp(17.62*Tc/(Tc+243.12)) * 100   [hPa -> Pa]\n      e    = RH * es\n      ppb  = (e/p) * 1e9\nwith RH = GEOSFP A3dyn RH, p = GEOSFP P and T = GEOSFP I3 T -- here Pc and Tc respectively. 611.2 below is the 6.112 hPa coefficient already in Pa.\n\nDrives BOTH FastJX.H2O (the O(1D)+H2O branching behind j_o32OH) and SuperFast.H2O (the H2O+NO2 reaction and the HO2+HO2 water enhancement), exactly as the reference couples both. A constant tropospheric value here is not merely inaccurate, it is unstable: the O(1D)->2OH branching is a function of the H2O MIXING RATIO alone, so a fixed 1.84% overstates the OH yield ~3,300x above 12 km and drove a HOx runaway that killed the solver 20 simulated minutes into a dawn run at NLEV=72."
        },
